 89	\begin{definition}[Polynomials of low individual degree]
 90	Let $q$ be a prime power,
 91	and let $m$ and $d$ be nonnegative integers.
 92	We define $\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$ to be the set of polynomials in $\F_q^m$
 93	with individual degree~$d$.
 94	\end{definition}
 95	
 96	\begin{remark}
 97	Note that as defined in \Cref{sec:intro},
 98	in this work a polynomial with individual degree~$d$ is one in which the degree~$d_i$ of each coordinate~$i$
 99	is \emph{at most~$d$}.
100	This allows us to say ``individual degree~$d$'' rather than the wordier ``individual degree at most~$d$''.
101	For example, under this definition, $\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$ is contained in $\polyfunc{m}{q}{d+1}$ for each~$d$.
102	\end{remark}
103	
104	The most important fact about low-degree polynomials is that they have large distance from each other.
105	This is shown by the following lemma.
106	
107	\begin{lemma}[Schwartz-Zippel lemma~\cite{Sch80,Zip79}]\label{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}
108	Let $g, h:\F_q^m \rightarrow \F_q$ be two distinct polynomials of total degree~$d$.
109	Then
110	\begin{equation*}
111	\Pr_{\bx \sim \F_q^m}[g(\bx) = h(\bx)] \leq \frac{d}{q}.
112	\end{equation*}
113	\end{lemma}
